include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: a syscall handed more room than the buffer has.
--
-- `read` is told to put up to 4096 bytes somewhere, and `small` is 16 of them.
-- The kernel writes what it is told to write; it has never seen the buffer and
-- cannot find where it ends. So this smashes the frame with whatever arrives on
-- the descriptor -- a stack overflow driven by input, from two literals sitting
-- one line apart.
--
-- Nothing downstream can catch it. A syscall is inline assembly with a "memory"
-- clobber, and a clobber says SOMETHING changed, not this buffer and that many
-- bytes -- so GCC stays silent at every warning level, including `-fanalyzer`.
--
-- The requirement is an ordinary contract clause on the primitive itself,
-- `ensure capacity <= buffer.size`, and which side it constrains is DERIVED:
-- a clause on the OUT port is a promise about the result and is checked at run
-- time, while one on an IN port is a requirement on the call and is decided
-- here. Same keyword, no run-time cost -- the corpus is byte-identical with the
-- clauses added.

program goes
  small is 16 bytes
  n is 0
  input is already linux.file (descriptor is 0)
  input.read (buffer is small, capacity is 4096, count is n)
end
